# Grants the solar-monitor service account passwordless sudo for exactly one
# script -- the pi-control helper -- which itself only accepts a small,
# validated set of actions (reboot, restart-service, governor, wifi-scan,
# wifi-connect). No other command is ever run as root.
#
# The rule grants root to whatever that file contains, so it must name a
# copy the service account cannot rewrite. Install one, owned by root:
#
#   sudo install -o root -g root -m 0755 deploy/pi-control.sh /usr/local/sbin/solar-monitor-pi-control
#
# The app uses that installed copy whenever it exists, and re-running the
# install after an update refreshes it. Pointing the rule at deploy/ in the
# checkout instead is safe only when the checkout and every directory above
# it are owned by root -- never for a checkout in the service user's home
# directory, where anything running as that user could replace the script
# and then run it as root.
#
# Then install this rule, adjusting the username if yours differs:
#   sudo cp deploy/solar-monitor-sudoers.example /etc/sudoers.d/solar-monitor
#   sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/solar-monitor
#   sudo visudo -c
solar-monitor ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/sbin/solar-monitor-pi-control
